# Fixtures no test device can provide, written by hand after these formats:
#
# sys/class/hwmon/hwmon0, links.json
#   A Nuvoton NCT6779D Super-I/O as on x86 routers: temperatures with labels and
#   PWM outputs. Attribute names and units: the kernel's
#   Documentation/hwmon/sysfs-interface.rst; the chip name, temperature source
#   labels and platform device (nct6775.656): Documentation/hwmon/nct6775.rst
#   and drivers/hwmon/nct6775-core.c. Its fans (fanN_input, _min, _alarm; fan2
#   stopped below its minimum, so in alarm) and voltages (inN_input, _min,
#   _max, _alarm, in millivolts after the driver's scaling), with the _beep
#   and _pulses files the driver has besides: sysfs-interface.rst's "Fans"
#   and "Voltages", and the attributes nct6775.rst lists. Its two first
#   temperatures' limits: tempN_max and tempN_max_hyst (80 and 75 degrees)
#   and tempN_alarm, as sysfs-interface.rst's "Temperatures" has them.
# sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1
#   A chip with neither a name file nor a device link, below 0 degrees.
# sys/class/hwmon/hwmon2, links.json
#   A TI INA219 current and power monitor on I2C bus 0 at 0x40, as on PoE and
#   battery boards, with a 10 mOhm shunt: shunt voltage (in0_input, mV), bus
#   voltage (in1_input, mV), current (curr1_input, mA) and power
#   (power1_input, uW), and shunt_resistor and update_interval: the
#   attributes of Documentation/hwmon/ina2xx.rst, the units of
#   sysfs-interface.rst's "Voltages", "Currents" and "Power"; the device path
#   as an I2C client's (bus-address) under its adapter.
# sys/class/hwmon/hwmon3, links.json
#   A MediaTek MT7915-family radio (mt76's mt7915 driver, which the MT7981's
#   Wi-Fi uses) with its throttling limits: temp1_input, temp1_crit and
#   temp1_max, the driver's 110 and 100 degrees (MT7915_CRIT_TEMP,
#   MT7915_MAX_TEMP in mt7915.h; the attributes of mt7915_thermal_init() in
#   init.c). The access point's captures hold its radios' temp1_input only.
# sys/class/hwmon/hwmon4, links.json
#   A TI LM25066 hot-swap controller on I2C bus 1 at 0x10, as on PoE boards:
#   power1_input and its average (power1_average), power1_input_highest and
#   power1_alarm, which the collector does not export, and curr1_input and
#   in1_input of the same 12 V input. Attributes: Documentation/hwmon/lm25066.rst;
#   units: sysfs-interface.rst's "Power" (uW).
# sys/class/thermal/thermal_zone0
#   A filogic SoC's cpu-thermal with its trip points, which the devices'
#   captures do not hold: critical at 125 degrees, hot at 120, and active at
#   115, 85 and 60 for the fan, in the order of OpenWrt's mediatek/filogic
#   device trees. Attributes: Documentation/driver-api/thermal/sysfs-api.rst,
#   "Thermal zone device sys I/F" (trip_point_[0-*]_type and _temp, in
#   millidegrees).
# sys/class/thermal/cooling_device{0,1,2}
#   The cooling devices of an ARM board with a fan and an MT7915-family radio,
#   which neither device's captures hold: a CPU's frequency steps
#   (cpufreq-cpu0, of drivers/thermal/cpufreq_cooling.c), a PWM fan at its
#   first of four steps (pwm-fan, drivers/hwmon/pwm-fan.c) and the radio's
#   transmit throttling, named after its wiphy (phy0; max_state 99,
#   MT7915_CDEV_THROTTLE_MAX of mt76's mt7915.h). Attributes:
#   Documentation/driver-api/thermal/sysfs-api.rst, "Cooling device attributes".
# commands/ethtool_{eth0,lan1,phy0-ap0}.txt, commands/ethtool_i_{eth0,lan1,phy0-ap0}.txt
#   `ethtool <device>` and `ethtool -i <device>` (ethtool 6.x, netlink) of
#   what the devices' ethtool -S captures are of, which no capture holds: a
#   Realtek RTL8125B (r8169, 10 Mb/s to 2.5 Gb/s over twisted pair) linked at
#   1 Gb/s to a partner without 2.5G, the r8169 firmware's name and date; a
#   DSA port of an MT7531 switch with no link, advertising no pause frames;
#   and a radio's interface, whose mac80211 driver reports no link modes.
#   Layout: dump_link_modes(), dump_pause() and the "Supported ports: [ "
#   of ethtool's netlink/settings.c (the tab after the first port is its
#   own), and do_gdrv()'s "key: value" lines (ethtool.c); the r8169's
#   firmware file name (rtl_chip_infos in r8169_main.c), mac80211's
#   driver, version and bus-info (ieee80211_get_drvinfo() in
#   net/mac80211/ethtool.c, the wiphy's parent device).
# proc/interrupts
#   An MT7981 access point's (two Cortex-A53s, a GICv3, OpenWrt 25.12's 6.12),
#   which neither device's captures hold: the timer, UART, SPI, I2C, the
#   Ethernet frame engine's two queues, the switch, USB, the radio, and a GPIO
#   button on the pinctrl's EINT. Layout: show_interrupts() in
#   kernel/irq/proc.c (a "%*d:" name, "%10u " per CPU, the chip, its hwirq,
#   the trigger and the actions' names) and arm64's arch_show_interrupts()
#   in arch/arm64/kernel/smp.c (IPI0 to IPI7 and their descriptions, and Err
#   with a single count).
# proc/pressure/{cpu,io,memory,irq}
#   Pressure stall information of a kernel with CONFIG_PSI and
#   CONFIG_IRQ_TIME_ACCOUNTING (6.12, OpenWrt 25.12's), which neither device's
#   captures hold, after a day's uptime: a `some` and a `full` line of cpu,
#   io and memory (cpu's full is 0 system-wide), and irq's `full` alone.
#   Format: psi_show() in kernel/sched/psi.c ("%s avg10=%lu.%02lu avg60=...
#   total=%llu", microseconds); Documentation/accounting/psi.rst.
# proc/diskstats, sys/block/sda/queue/rotational
#   Kernel 4.14's /proc/diskstats (OpenWrt 19.07's), 14 fields a line: before
#   the discard statistics (4.18) and the flush ones (5.5). Format:
#   diskstats_show() in block/genhd.c of 4.14, fields as
#   Documentation/iostats.txt of 4.14 lists them. A NAND router with mtdblock
#   partitions, its rootfs on a ubiblock, zram swap, a loop device and a USB
#   hard disk (sda, rotational) with one partition.
# var/prometheus/*.prom
#   Files as node_exporter's textfile collector reads them: Prometheus text
#   exposition format 0.0.4 (prometheus.io/docs/instrumenting/exposition_formats);
#   truncated.prom is one whose writer died mid-line, which is why node_exporter
#   recommends writing to a temporary file and renaming it.
# sockets/unbound.stats_noreset.txt
#   Unbound 1.26.1's (OpenWrt 25.12's) reply to `UBCT1 stats_noreset` on its
#   control socket, what `unbound-control stats_noreset` prints: num-threads 2
#   (the UCI's num_threads), no dnscrypt, and extended-statistics (the UCI's
#   extended_stats '1'; OpenWrt's default is '0', which ends the reply at
#   time.elapsed). Lines and number formats: daemon/remote.c's do_stats(),
#   print_stats(), print_uptime(), and with extended-statistics print_mem(),
#   print_hist() (40 buckets of util/timehist.c, doubling from 1 us, each a
#   range: the recursion times of the 6106 recursive replies) and print_ext()
#   (the types, classes, opcodes and rcodes no query had left out, as
#   stat_inhibit_zero does but for rcodes 0 to 5; the queries' types and
#   rcodes summing to total.num.queries); the totals as daemon/stats.c's
#   server_stats_add() makes them (sums, the maximum for the .max counters,
#   the mean of the thread medians); the counters' meaning:
#   doc/unbound-control.rst, "Statistic Counters".
# ubus/network.interface.dump.json
#   netifd's `ubus call network.interface dump` with what neither device has:
#   an interface taken down (guest), one without its device and with an error
#   (lte), a DHCP client still pending (wwan), PPPoE (wan) and DHCPv6 with a
#   delegated prefix (wan6) assigned to lan, with a ULA and an assignment
#   without a local address. Fields and when each is there: netifd_dump_status(),
#   interface_ip_dump_prefix_list() and interface_ip_dump_prefix_assignment_list()
#   in netifd's ubus.c (cbb83a18, OpenWrt 25.12's netifd).
# ubus/dhcp.ipv4leases.json, ubus/dhcp.ipv6leases.json
#   odhcpd serving DHCPv4 (odhcpd, not the default odhcpd-ipv6only) and DHCPv6
#   on the dump's lan: an IA_NA with an address in each prefix and an IA_PD.
#   Layout: handle_dhcpv4_leases() and handle_dhcpv6_leases() in odhcpd's
#   src/ubus.c (5d7be43f, OpenWrt 25.12's odhcpd); (uint32_t)-1 is "infinite",
#   which ucode's ubus gives as -1, as it gives every blobmsg_add_u32() signed.
# commands/tc_s_j_qdisc_show.json
#   `tc -s -j qdisc show` (iproute2 6.18, OpenWrt 25.12's tc-tiny) on a PPPoE
#   router with what neither device has: SQM's layer_cake (a diffserv4 CAKE on
#   pppoe-wan, its four tins Bulk, Best Effort, Video and Voice, with a backlog
#   and the ACK filter on) and its ingress side shaped by simple.qos (HTB over
#   fq_codel on the ifb). Keys and units: print_qdisc() in tc/tc_qdisc.c and
#   print_tcstats2_attr() in tc/tc_util.c (the generic counters),
#   cake_print_opt(), cake_print_xstats() and cake_print_json_tin() in
#   tc/q_cake.c (rates in bytes per second, delays in microseconds),
#   fq_codel_print_xstats() in tc/q_fq_codel.c; the tins' rates as
#   sch_cake.c's cake_config_diffserv4() splits the bandwidth.
# commands/chronyc_c_tracking_unsynchronised.txt
#   Not written by hand: `chronyc -c tracking` of a real chronyd 4.8 (OpenWrt
#   25.12's chrony package) under procd in the pkgcheck container, without a
#   network, so with no source: what chronyd answers before it first syncs.
# commands/chronyc_c_sources.txt
#   `chronyc -c sources` (chrony 4.8) with what the devices' two servers are
#   not: a selected GPS reference clock, a peer, a server never reached (its
#   last sample (uint32_t)-1 seconds ago, a polling interval under a second), a
#   falseticker and a jittery one. Fields and characters: process_cmd_sources()
#   and print_report() in chrony's client.c (reachability in octal).
# var/run/prometheus-exporter/timesync/ntpd
#   What /etc/hotplug.d/ntp/90-prometheus-exporter keeps of busybox ntpd's
#   event: a periodic one of a synchronized ntpd at stratum 3. The environment
#   busybox 1.37 (OpenWrt 25.12's) gives /usr/sbin/ntpd-hotplug: run_script()
#   in networking/ntpd.c (stratum, freq_drift_ppm, poll_interval, offset as
#   %f); ACTION is ntpd-hotplug's argument. A real ntpd without a network (a
#   25.12 container) sends `periodic` with stratum=16, offset=0.000000.
# uci/dhcp.json
#   /etc/config/dhcp as OpenWrt 25.12's dnsmasq and odhcpd packages ship it,
#   for the dump's lan and wan: DHCPv4 on lan, from its 100th address for 150,
#   which odhcpd serves too (dhcpv4 'server'), and none on wan.
# tmp/dhcp.leases
#   A dnsmasq lease file for that lan with DHCPv6 leases too: an infinite (0)
#   and an expired lease, one outside every subnet, a temporary (T) IPv6
#   address, and the agent-info line dnsmasq appends for relayed clients.
#   Layout: lease_update_file() in dnsmasq's src/lease.c (2.93).
# commands/ubnt-manager-l.txt
#   What `ubnt-manager -l` prints for the radios of the two
#   commands/ubnt-manager-j-t-*.json captures, as the collector's unit test has
#   it; the documentation's metrics reference runs every collector on this set.
